# Auto detect text files and perform LF normalization
* text=auto

# Execution-sensitive scripts must be LF (run in bash/CI). Covers the relocated tools/ kits.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The gate leg manifest is runtime-read by run-gates.sh (a stray CR would corrupt leg names/argv).
tools/gate-legs.json text eol=lf
# The size gate's high-water record: a data file the gate PARSES and `--bump` REWRITES. The gate
# strips CR from the value so it is tolerant either way, but an unpinned record makes every --bump
# rewrite the whole file on a CRLF checkout, and the key side has no such tolerance to spare.
tools/template-size-highwater.txt text eol=lf
# The DECLARED ceilings, same reason: the gate parses this file, and an unpinned path smudges to
# CRLF on this fleet. A person edits it, so it never gets a rewrite to normalise it back.
tools/template-size-limits.txt text eol=lf

# Git hooks are extensionless (the *.sh rule above misses them); a CRLF hook won't execute.
.githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf
.githooks/pre-push text eol=lf

# memory-tree determinism (this repo dogfoods the kit). THREE gates read these bytes, and all three
# are byte- or char-exact:
#   check 9  byte-compares the generated build index against a fresh LF render — an unpinned
#            generated file on a Windows checkout differs on EVERY line, so it reds on a file nobody
#            touched and goes green only right after a render;
#   check 6  caps index files at a DECLARED byte bound (`ROW_DOC_CAP_BYTES` / `DOSSIER_CAP_BYTES`);
#   check 7  caps an index ENTRY at 300 chars.
# A CRLF checkout adds one byte to every LINE, so checks 6 and 7 measure something different per
# platform: the same corpus passes on Linux and fails on Windows. Measured — DECISIONS.md at
# 20466 B (LF) reported 20559 B against the 20480 B cap in the primary tree and blocked a push,
# while the worktree that wrote it read green. AGENTS.md already states LF for "the memory-tree data
# files"; this is that claim made true.
#
# Pinned by EXTENSION rather than by listing today's index files, because the index set is DERIVED
# (`check-memory-hygiene.sh --print-index-set`): a new backlog shard, guide, or build README joins
# it with no edit here. A pin that must be remembered is the pin that gets forgotten.
memory/**/*.md text eol=lf
memory/**/*.toml text eol=lf
memory/**/*.txt text eol=lf

# The two AUTHORED indexes every node appends to. `merge=rows` routes a conflict through the
# row-keyed driver (tools/memory-tree/merge-rows.py), which merges by record id: an append-collision
# between two nodes auto-resolves with both rows present and no duplicate id. Deliberately NOT
# `merge=union` — union never loses an id but introduced a duplicate in 147 of 151 historical
# DECISIONS.md conflicts upstream, and these files hold zero duplicate ids.
# No `eol=lf` pin here on purpose: `* text=auto` above already normalises the committed blobs, the
# driver is newline-preserving by construction, and a pin would rewrite every node's worktree bytes
# on its next checkout for nothing. A node with no `merge.rows.driver` configured falls back to git's
# built-in three-way text merge with a warning — the pre-change behaviour, which is why the attribute
# and the per-node config can land in one commit. `bash tools/check-wiring.sh --fix` configures it.
memory/DECISIONS.md merge=rows
memory/backlog/*.md merge=rows

# The governance template is under a STRICT byte gate — pin it (+ companions) to LF so a CRLF
# checkout can't inflate the count past the limit. (The gate also normalizes, belt-and-suspenders.)
parallel-coding-governance*.md text eol=lf

# The rendered recall Skill is compared to a fresh render with plain `diff` by
# `adopt-memory-recall.sh --check`. The renderer always emits LF, so without this pin a Windows
# checkout turns the file CRLF and the leg reds on EVERY line — while passing whenever the working
# copy happens to have just been rendered. That is green-by-accident, not a gate (§7).
.claude/skills/memory-recall/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The rendered drift-audit Skill, same reason. Belt-and-braces rather than load-bearing here:
# `adopt-drift-audit.sh --check` strips CR on BOTH sides before diffing, so it cannot red on a CRLF
# checkout. The pin keeps the committed bytes deterministic anyway, and stops a future maintainer who
# drops that `tr -d '\r'` from reintroducing memory-recall's green-by-accident failure.
.claude/skills/drift-audit/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The unattended-run kit's rendered Skill, pinned for the identical reason as the two above. It is
# the THIRD instance of the shape, and the pin is also what puts it into `check-wiring.sh`'s eol
# population, which is DERIVED from tracked `.claude/` paths carrying this attribute — so the pin is
# the whole of that wiring and the script needs no edit.
.claude/skills/unattended/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The /session-kickoff engine. Pinned because a GATE now reads it — check 12 of the unattended leg
# greps its text, which nothing did before. That leg strips CR anyway, and both halves are the point:
# strip so the comparison is right, pin so the bytes are. Measured 2026-08-10: the engine came out of
# this worktree with 224 CR bytes, and every pattern in check 12 survived only because each happens
# to match mid-line. The first pattern anchored to a line end would have broken silently.
skills/session-kickoff/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# The workflow scripts. They are not byte-compared by any gate — `check-workflow-syntax.js` parses
# them and CRLF parses fine — so this pin is not about a gate. It is about the LAUNCHER: a workflow
# script is INLINED into the tool call that runs it, and the permission layer refuses a payload
# carrying control characters. Measured 2026-08-10 in a linked worktree: `tier2-review.js` came out
# of the checkout with 350 CR bytes and the shipped Tier-2 harness could not be launched at all,
# while its own syntax leg stayed green. The committed blobs were already LF; `* text=auto` plus
# core.autocrlf smudged every unpinned path on the way out.
tools/workflows/*.js text eol=lf

# codebase-map determinism (this repo dogfoods the kit). The gate's freshness leg BYTE-COMPARES the
# generated artifacts against a fresh LF render, so an unpinned generated file on a Windows checkout
# differs on EVERY line — the same green-only-right-after-a-render failure the memory-tree pins above
# exist for. The conf is pinned because it is SOURCED BY BASH: a CRLF checkout leaves stray carriage
# returns in every value on Linux.
.codebase-map.conf text eol=lf

# The memory-tree conf, pinned for the identical reason as `.codebase-map.conf` above: it is SOURCED
# BY BASH (`. "$ROOT/.memory-tree.conf"`, check-memory-hygiene.sh) with no CR stripping. It was
# unpinned and CRLF in this worktree, and the suite still passed only because MSYS/git-bash strips CR
# on source — the same node-specific behaviour that makes a CRLF fixture untestable here. On Linux or
# CI every value would carry a trailing CR and MEMORY_ROOT would resolve to `memory\r`. AGENTS.md
# already claims LF for "the memory-tree data files"; this is that claim made true for the conf. The
# shipped example is pinned too, because the adopter copies it to the sourced path verbatim.
.memory-tree.conf text eol=lf
tools/memory-tree/.memory-tree.conf.example text eol=lf

# The unattended-run kit's conf, pinned for the identical reason as the two above: it is SOURCED BY
# BASH by the driver and by the gate leg, with no CR stripping. Its values are shell COMMANDS
# (`LANDER`, `GATE_CMD`, `WIRING_CHECK`) — a trailing CR on a CRLF checkout does not merely dirty a
# string here, it appends a carriage return to the command the run lands with.
.unattended.conf text eol=lf
tools/unattended/.unattended.conf.example text eol=lf
# The shipped protocol is BYTE-COMPARED against this repo's installed copy under `memory/guides/`,
# which `memory/**/*.md` already pins. A gate that byte-compares needs BOTH halves — the pin so the
# committed bytes are right, and CR normalisation in the comparison so a Windows checkout does not
# red every line — and the shipped half had only the second.
tools/unattended/*.md text eol=lf

# The memory-tree kit's three shipped templates, pinned for the IDENTICAL reason as the line above
# and found the same way: `kit-dogfood-parity.test.sh` renders the template and byte-compares it to
# the installed copy under `memory/`, which `memory/**/*.md` already pins — so the gate had one
# pinned half and one unpinned half. Both were LF when this landed, and `git add` warned that the
# next checkout would smudge the template to CRLF, at which point the render emits CRLF, the live
# side is CR-stripped, and the leg reds on every line while passing whenever a render just ran. The
# comparison now strips CR on both sides too; the pin is what keeps the committed bytes right.
tools/memory-tree/*.template.md text eol=lf

memory/map/generated/* text eol=lf
memory/map/baseline.toml text eol=lf
memory/map/affordance-exempt.toml text eol=lf

# govkit — the deployer's registry and its per-entry descriptors. Pinned for the same reason every
# other declaration file here is: these are READ by a tool, and a CR byte in a conf or a descriptor
# is a class this repo has already paid for twice.
tools/govkit/*.toml text eol=lf
tools/govkit/*.py text eol=lf
tools/govkit/entries/*.toml text eol=lf
tools/*/kit.toml text eol=lf

# The lexicon kit's conf. Pinned for the same determinism reason as the two confs above: it is read
# by the kit's Python reader AND grepped by `adopt-lexicon.sh`, whose `ratified` extraction strips a
# trailing quote with `s/"$//` — a CRLF checkout puts a carriage return after that quote and the
# anchor stops matching, leaving `"
# `, a NON-EMPTY value. That INVERTS the unratified-seed refusal:
# the one check stopping an uncurated verb table from reaching the merge bar passes exactly when it
# must fire. Not harmless, and MEASURED — this comment previously said "harmless today", which the
# same kit's own selftest then disproved. The reader also strips CR; both halves are armed.
.lexicon.conf text eol=lf
